 @D00ML0RD1  Not that I would object to making purchase by bots illegal but I can see this clogging the court system so badly that they would not be able to handle serious crimes that need handling ASAP.  I agree that the stores and online vendors could do a LOT more than they have on this issue.  I am still scratching my head over why don't they have a sign up to purchase sheet on the vendor's web sites, open it to sign ups, and then fill the orders from the oldest to the newest signups as the cards come in.  No muss, no fuss, and a few billion hours saved by tens if not hundreds of millions of people wanting new GPUs.  Limiting these cards to one purchaser would be good too.  Yes, I know that some families and even individuals have multiple PCs.  I have 5 of them myself and my wife has 2 of her own.  But one per household would ensure that more people get at least 1 card, at least until manufacturing catches up with the massive demand for these products.

In store buys are good but would not be good for me because I live about 50 miles from the nearest Best Buy and there is no other major computer parts store within hundreds of miles of here.  Online buying has worked very well for me... until 2020 hit.  Then the supply chain completely flopped.  I'm sure that it will recover but it seems agonizingly slow at times.  

I see that AMD's stock share price has dropped from around $95 down to around $79.  Nvidia is also down somewhat.  I suspect that it is this manufacturing problem that is crimping their earnings and causing their stock share prices to fall.

